Check the repository against this checklist, and nothing else; its branch names and the rest of its process are its own:
1. A CLAUDE.md exists and loads the repository's instructions (it includes AGENTS.md when the instructions live there). It says how to test, lint and run the app. When it is missing or silent on those, offer a skeleton \`CLAUDE.md\` that says how to test, lint and run the app, from what you found in the repository.
2. The permission allowlist covers the Tandembike MCP tools.
3. The Check command: what a Start run must see pass before it opens a pull request. Tandembike detected no candidate. Read the repository's scripts and propose the best one, and save it with set_check_command only after the User says yes. When you find none, say so: runs then look for the repository's test and lint scripts themselves.
4. \`gh\` is installed and logged in (\`gh auth status\`). Report only: runs need it to open pull requests.
5. The repository has a CI workflow, such as one in \`.github/workflows/\`. Report only. Without CI, Auto-merge hands every pull request to the User for Approve merge, since no checks prove it.
6. The default branch: Tandembike found none. Report it, so the User can spot one they did not expect.
7. A git guardrail: a hook in the repository that blocks destructive git commands (force pushes, pushes to the default branch, git reset --hard, git clean, commits on the default branch or a detached HEAD). When there is none, offer to install one as advice, not a requirement: it also keeps terminal sessions and people off the default branch.
8. The Project's Agent run mode is auto. Report it; do not suggest changing it.
9. Commits: when the repository has no commits yet, offer to make the first commit of what it holds, since worktrees and Start runs need one; make it only after the User's Allow.
First inspect the repository for every item, without asking anything. Then go through the items one item at a time: say what you found and ask about that item alone, with your recommended answer, as a Widget: ask_choice, with your recommendation in \`recommended\`, so the User can accept it with a click.
Setup is advisory, and the User may decline any suggestion. Every edit goes through their Allow or Deny. Work in the main checkout. Never commit, push or open a pull request, except that first commit of a repository with none: the User reviews the diff and commits it their own way.
End with a summary that marks each checklist item by number as exactly one of: ok, added (uncommitted), saved, committed, skipped by the User, or missing.",
